            <ArticleTitle>Testicular Estrogen-Secreting Leydig Cell Tumor in 18F-FDG PET/CT: An Incidental Detection in a Patient Treated by Chemotherapy for Hodgkin Lymphoma.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>41-43</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/RLU.0000000000001905</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We present images of a 50-year-old man who referred for treatment of a classic Hodgkin lymphoma. While F-FDG PET/CT demonstrated a complete metabolic remission after chemotherapy, an increased F-FDG uptake of a right testicular lesion in F-FDG PET/CT and an unexplained bilateral gynecomastia were observed. A benign Leydig cell tumor was histopathologically proved after a right radical orchiectomy. The serum estradiol level was abnormally elevated reflecting the estrogen-secreting profile. This report highlights that a focal F-FDG uptake in the testicular region with unexplained gynecomastia should suggest the diagnosis of an estrogen-secreting Leydig cell tumor on F-FDG PET/CT.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
